-- APPLIED TO PRODUCTION 2026-08-20 via Supabase MCP as
--   `fn_rakeback_recompute_periods_fix_1000_row_truncation`
--   `fn_rakeback_recompute_periods_drop_window_sort`
-- Mirror only.
--
-- ═══ CRITICAL: players were under-paid rakeback ═══
--
-- The settler recomputed each week's rake_generated by FETCHING the club's
-- rake_records for that week (`.limit(50000)`) and summing shares in
-- JavaScript. PostgREST caps a response at 1000 rows, so it only ever saw
-- ~1000 records of a week that holds 81,000-180,000. rake_generated was
-- therefore computed from well under 1% of the real data.
--
-- Proven on live pending periods before the fix:
--   user 1c1c12c2…   stored 23.17   actual 377.01   (16x understated)
--
-- Worse than a proportional shortfall, because rake_generated also selects the
-- rakeback TIER (5/10/15/20/30% at 100/500/2000/10000): that player sat in the
-- 5% band when they belonged in 10%, compounding the loss on money owed.
--
-- The cap cannot be raised from the client, so the computation moved into the
-- database. The arithmetic is replicated exactly, not approximated:
--   * equalShareCents — base = floor(total_cents / n), first `remainder`
--     contributors get one extra cent. "First" is the order the engine sees:
--     Object.entries() over the parsed JSON, i.e. jsonb's stored key order.
--     jsonb sorts keys by (length, bytes) and every key is a 36-char UUID, so
--     it reduces to lexicographic — what ORDER BY on the key text gives.
--   * n counts only contributors with a POSITIVE contribution, matching the
--     engine's `.filter(([, amt]) => Number(amt) > 0)`.
--   * the tier ladder matches RAKEBACK_TIERS and fn_close_settlement_period.
--   * paid/expired weeks stay immutable (DO UPDATE gated on status='pending').
--   * users already holding a period row under a DIFFERENT club are skipped —
--     rakeback_periods has a second unique key (user_id, period_start) that
--     spans all clubs, so those inserts already 409'd; skipping keeps behaviour
--     identical while letting the set-based insert finish.
--
-- The first version ranked contributors with a window function, which sorted
-- every share row (~522,000 for the busiest club-week) and exceeded a 2-minute
-- statement timeout. Replaced with a correlated count — same value, no global
-- sort; the busiest club-week (180,261 records -> 522,238 shares, 540 users)
-- now completes comfortably.
